concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-6q a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "6Q Meaning of default information"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-6q ;
    values:originalText "Consumer credit defaults  (1) Default information about an individual is information about a payment (including a payment that is wholly or partly a payment of interest) that the individual is overdue in making in relation to consumer credit that has been provided by a credit provider to the individual if:  (a) the individual is at least 60 days overdue in making the payment; and  (b) the provider has given a written notice to the individual informing the individual of the overdue payment and requesting that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment; and  (d) the amount of the overdue payment is equal to or more than:  (i) $150; or  (ii) such higher amount as is prescribed by the regulations. Guarantor defaults  (2) Default information about an individual is information about a payment that the individual is overdue in making as a guarantor under a guarantee given against any default by a person (the borrower) in repaying all or any of the debt deferred under consumer credit provided by a credit provider to the borrower if: ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Schedule 2  Credit reporting  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012  Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  (a) the provider has given the individual written notice of the borrower’s default that gave rise to the individual’s obligation to make the overdue payment; and  (b) the notice requests that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) at least 60 days have passed since the day on which the notice was given; and  (d) in addition to giving the notice, the provider has taken other steps to recover the amount of the overdue payment from the individual; and  (e) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment." ;
    skos:definition "Consumer credit defaults  (1) Default information about an individual is information about a payment (including a payment that is wholly or partly a payment of interest) that the individual is overdue in making in relation to consumer credit that has been provided by a credit provider to the individual if:  (a) the individual is at least 60 days overdue in making the payment; and  (b) the provider has given a written notice to the individual informing the individual of the overdue payment and requesting that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment; and  (d) the amount of the overdue payment is equal to or more than:  (i) $150; or  (ii) such higher amount as is prescribed by the regulations. Guarantor defaults  (2) Default information about an individual is information about a payment that the individual is overdue in making as a guarantor under a guarantee given against any default by a person (the borrower) in repaying all or any of the debt deferred under consumer credit provided by a credit provider to the borrower if: ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Schedule 2  Credit reporting  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012  Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  (a) the provider has given the individual written notice of the borrower’s default that gave rise to the individual’s obligation to make the overdue payment; and  (b) the notice requests that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) at least 60 days have passed since the day on which the notice was given; and  (d) in addition to giving the notice, the provider has taken other steps to recover the amount of the overdue payment from the individual; and  (e) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "0cced1191b1905f16761ba1b0be50feafdab20f8bdc87331082bdc7bbdad25f0" ;
    cof:pageNumber "73"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-6q-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-6q-ss-2 ;
    cml:curationStatus cml:Proposed ;
    cml:schemaVersion "2" ;
    cml:proposedBy <https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml> ;
    prov:wasDerivedFrom <privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf> ;
    dc:source "privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012.pdf, p.73" .
doc:sch-2-sec-6q a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "6Q Meaning of default information" ;
    values:kind "section" ;
    values:originalText "Consumer credit defaults  (1) Default information about an individual is information about a payment (including a payment that is wholly or partly a payment of interest) that the individual is overdue in making in relation to consumer credit that has been provided by a credit provider to the individual if:  (a) the individual is at least 60 days overdue in making the payment; and  (b) the provider has given a written notice to the individual informing the individual of the overdue payment and requesting that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment; and  (d) the amount of the overdue payment is equal to or more than:  (i) $150; or  (ii) such higher amount as is prescribed by the regulations. Guarantor defaults  (2) Default information about an individual is information about a payment that the individual is overdue in making as a guarantor under a guarantee given against any default by a person (the borrower) in repaying all or any of the debt deferred under consumer credit provided by a credit provider to the borrower if: ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Schedule 2  Credit reporting  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012  Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  (a) the provider has given the individual written notice of the borrower’s default that gave rise to the individual’s obligation to make the overdue payment; and  (b) the notice requests that the individual pay the amount of the overdue payment; and  (c) at least 60 days have passed since the day on which the notice was given; and  (d) in addition to giving the notice, the provider has taken other steps to recover the amount of the overdue payment from the individual; and  (e) the provider is not prevented by a statute of limitations from recovering the amount of the overdue payment." ;
    cof:pageNumber "73"^^xsd:integer .

