concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20d a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "20D Dealing with unsolicited credit information"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-20d ;
    values:originalText "(1) If:  (a) a credit reporting body receives credit information about an individual; and  (b) the body did not solicit the information; the body must, within a reasonable period after receiving the information, determine whether or not the body could have collected the information under section 20C if the body had solicited the information.  (2) The credit reporting body may use or disclose the credit information for the purposes of making the determination under subsection (1).  (3) If the credit reporting body determines that it could have collected the credit information, sections 20E to 20ZA apply in relation to the information as if the body had collected the information under section 20C.  (4) If the credit reporting body determines that it could not have collected the credit information, the body must, as soon as practicable, destroy the information. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Schedule 2  Credit reporting  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012  Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  Civil penalty: 1,000 penalty units.  (5) Subsection (4) does not apply if the credit reporting body is required by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order, to retain the credit information. Subdivision D—Dealing with credit reporting information etc." ;
    skos:definition "(1) If:  (a) a credit reporting body receives credit information about an individual; and  (b) the body did not solicit the information; the body must, within a reasonable period after receiving the information, determine whether or not the body could have collected the information under section 20C if the body had solicited the information.  (2) The credit reporting body may use or disclose the credit information for the purposes of making the determination under subsection (1).  (3) If the credit reporting body determines that it could have collected the credit information, sections 20E to 20ZA apply in relation to the information as if the body had collected the information under section 20C.  (4) If the credit reporting body determines that it could not have collected the credit information, the body must, as soon as practicable, destroy the information. ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Schedule 2  Credit reporting  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012  Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  Civil penalty: 1,000 penalty units.  (5) Subsection (4) does not apply if the credit reporting body is required by or under an Australian law, or a court/tribunal order, to retain the credit information. Subdivision D—Dealing with credit reporting information etc."@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "5a6a23c1ce91242f7259332ae2d2c2f30115dd9d22e099b3bba3119c40508cf6" ;
    cof:pageNumber "83"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20d-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20d-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20d-ss-3 ;
