doc:sch-2-sec-20j-ss-2 a cof:Section ;
    cof:title "20J(2) Destruction of pre-screening assessment" ;
    values:kind "subsection" ;
    values:originalText "(2) If the entity is an APP entity but not a credit reporting body, Australian Privacy Principle 11.2 does not apply to the entity in relation to the pre-screening assessment." ;
    cof:pageNumber "89"^^xsd:integer .

concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20k a cml:Concept ;
    skos:prefLabel "20K No use or disclosure of credit reporting information during a"@en ;
    cml:realizedBy doc:sch-2-sec-20k ;
    values:originalText "ban period  (1) If:  (a) a credit reporting body holds credit reporting information about an individual; and  (b) the individual believes on reasonable grounds that the individual has been, or is likely to be, a victim of fraud (including identity fraud); and  (c) the individual requests the body not to use or disclose the information under this Division; then, despite any other provision of this Division, the body must not use or disclose the information during the ban period for the information. Civil penalty: 2,000 penalty units.  (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if:  (a) the individual expressly consents, in writing, to the use or disclosure of the credit reporting information under this Division; or  (b) the use or disclosure of the credit reporting information is required by or under an Australian law or a court/tribunal order. Ban period  (3) The ban period for credit reporting information about an individual is the period that:  (a) starts when the individual makes a request under paragraph (1)(c); and ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Credit reporting  Schedule 2  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012 Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  (b) ends:  (i) 21 days after the day on which the request is made; or  (ii) if the period is extended under subsection (4)—on the day after the extended period ends.  (4) If:  (a) there is a ban period for credit reporting information about an individual that is held by a credit reporting body; and  (b) before the ban period ends, the individual requests the body to extend that period; and  (c) the body believes on reasonable grounds that the individual has been, or is likely to be, a victim of fraud (including identity fraud); the body must:  (d) extend the ban period by such period as the body considers is reasonable in the circumstances; and  (e) give the individual written notification of the extension. Civil penalty: 1,000 penalty units.  (5) A ban period for credit reporting information may be extended more than once under subsection (4). No charge for request etc.  (6) If an individual makes a request under paragraph (1)(c) or (4)(b), a credit reporting body must not charge the individual for the making of the request or to give effect to the request." ;
    skos:definition "ban period  (1) If:  (a) a credit reporting body holds credit reporting information about an individual; and  (b) the individual believes on reasonable grounds that the individual has been, or is likely to be, a victim of fraud (including identity fraud); and  (c) the individual requests the body not to use or disclose the information under this Division; then, despite any other provision of this Division, the body must not use or disclose the information during the ban period for the information. Civil penalty: 2,000 penalty units.  (2) Subsection (1) does not apply if:  (a) the individual expressly consents, in writing, to the use or disclosure of the credit reporting information under this Division; or  (b) the use or disclosure of the credit reporting information is required by or under an Australian law or a court/tribunal order. Ban period  (3) The ban period for credit reporting information about an individual is the period that:  (a) starts when the individual makes a request under paragraph (1)(c); and ComLaw Authoritative Act C2015C00053  Credit reporting  Schedule 2  Privacy Amendment (Enhancing Privacy Protection) Act 2012 Compilation date: 12/3/14 Registered: 6/3/15  (b) ends:  (i) 21 days after the day on which the request is made; or  (ii) if the period is extended under subsection (4)—on the day after the extended period ends.  (4) If:  (a) there is a ban period for credit reporting information about an individual that is held by a credit reporting body; and  (b) before the ban period ends, the individual requests the body to extend that period; and  (c) the body believes on reasonable grounds that the individual has been, or is likely to be, a victim of fraud (including identity fraud); the body must:  (d) extend the ban period by such period as the body considers is reasonable in the circumstances; and  (e) give the individual written notification of the extension. Civil penalty: 1,000 penalty units.  (5) A ban period for credit reporting informati"@en ;
    cml:integrityHash "a061536bf5720b6a0b578ec679fb5df017b4e6f8c57f74dd4bcbf4e3cd1091cc" ;
    cof:pageNumber "90"^^xsd:integer ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20k-ss-1 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20k-ss-2 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20k-ss-3 ;
    cml:hasPart concept:privacy-amendment-enhancing-privacy-protection-act-2012-sch-2-sec-20k-ss-4 ;
